WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:52.000
<v Tove>This is Undercurrent, and I'm Tove Adler. A reader on the ninth floor of a building on Linden Hill wrote in to say her shower turns to a trickle every morning at seven. She asked whether that is her building's fault. It is not, quite.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:03:16.000
<v Rafi>Pressure at a tap is height you borrow. The water in the tower stands forty metres above the street, and every metre of it pushes. Your ninth floor is twenty-seven metres up, so you only get what is left over.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.000
<v Tove>And at seven in the morning, when everyone on the hill showers at once?

00:03:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
<v Rafi>The pipes lose some of it to friction on the way. Fast water loses more. That is the dip she feels.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:07:45.000
<v Tove>The tower on Linden Hill was built in 1931 for a neighbourhood of two-storey houses. We climbed it on a Tuesday with the operator who checks it every week.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:10:58.000
<v Rafi>At three in the morning almost nobody uses water, so whatever still flows is a leak. We measure it every night. On Linden Hill it is about four litres a second.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:13:05.000
<v Tove>From the mailbag: Jonas in Harbour Ward asks why his water tastes different in August. Warm reservoirs, a little more chlorine, and a switch to the river intake. Rafi has the long answer in Thursday's letter.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:14:20.000
<v Tove>Undercurrent is written and read by me, with sound by Ines Cardoso. Next time: what happens to the bins after the lorry turns the corner.
